          "Jeremiah was given the option of going to Babylon, where he would be treated well, or remaining in the land. He chose to stay with the people who remained. The prophet who had repeatedly warned Jerusalem about the coming disaster now lived among the survivors of that disaster.",
          "Another account in Jeremiah 40 describes Jeremiah being released at Ramah and entrusted to Gedaliah, whom the Babylonians appointed as governor over the people left in Judah. This was a fragile new beginning. The kingdom had fallen, but life had not completely disappeared from the land.",
