          "In Daniel 8, Daniel receives another vision involving a ram and a goat. The vision’s explanation identifies these figures with kingdoms and describes conflict, desecration and the suffering of the holy people. The chapter reflects the reality that political upheaval can threaten religious life and the worship of God.",
          "Daniel 10 introduces another vision. Daniel mourns, fasts and seeks understanding. He encounters a heavenly messenger whose appearance overwhelms him. The messenger explains that Daniel’s prayer has been heard, although the answer has involved a struggle beyond Daniel’s ordinary perception.",
          "The chapter presents the world as more complex than the visible movements of kings and armies. It uses heavenly imagery to describe conflict surrounding the rise and fall of kingdoms. Readers should treat these images within the literary and theological world of Daniel rather than using them to claim certainty about every unseen event in contemporary life.",
          "Daniel 11 describes conflict among powerful rulers, including rival kingdoms and a ruler who exalts himself and attacks the holy covenant. The chapter contains detailed political and military imagery. Many historical readings connect substantial portions of it with the conflicts involving the Hellenistic kingdoms after the death of Alexander the Great, particularly the Seleucid and Ptolemaic dynasties. Christian interpreters differ over how the later portions relate to future events.",
          "Daniel 12 brings the book to a climactic conclusion.",
          "The vision speaks of a time of distress, deliverance for those written in the book, and the awakening of those who sleep in the dust of the earth. Some are described as receiving everlasting life, while others face shame. The wise are portrayed as shining like the brightness of the heavens.",
          "The chapter also speaks of Daniel’s own future. He is told to go his way until the end, when he will rest and eventually receive his allotted inheritance.",
          "The conclusion does not give Daniel control over the future. It offers him a promise that his life and faithfulness belong within a story larger than his immediate circumstances.",
          "The book began with young exiles being taken to Babylon. It ends with a vision extending beyond the reign of any individual king. The empires that once appeared permanent are shown to be temporary. The people who suffer under them are not forgotten by God.",
          "Daniel’s prayer in chapter 9 and his visions in chapters 8–12 therefore belong together. Prayer brings the suffering of the present before God, while the visions express hope that present injustice and political upheaval will not define the final outcome.",
          "For Christians, Daniel’s visions also form part of the biblical background for the New Testament’s teaching about God’s kingdom, resurrection and final justice. Yet the details of prophetic chronology remain disputed, and Christian faithfulness should not depend on claiming certainty about every symbol or timetable.",
          "Daniel teaches readers to study Scripture carefully, confess wrongdoing honestly, pray persistently and remain faithful when the future is unclear.",
          "Interpretive note: Daniel 8–12 contains apocalyptic visions with complex historical references, symbols and numbers. Jewish and Christian interpreters differ over the dating, chronology and fulfilment of these passages. The story should distinguish the text’s explicit explanations from historical reconstructions and later interpretations. Avoid using the visions to announce unsupported dates for the end of the world or to identify contemporary groups as definitive enemies of God."
